(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the molecular mechanism of Pre-Replication Complex \(pre-RC\) licensing during G1 and explain how re-replication is prevented.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   DNA Replication Licensing and the Prevention of Re-Replication:) Tj
T*
(   1. Pre-Replication Complex \(pre-RC\) Assembly in G1 \(Licensing\):) Tj
T*
(   Origin licensing occurs strictly during late mitosis and G1 phase, a temporal window) Tj
T*
(   characterized by extremely low CDK activity:) Tj
T*
(   - Step 1 \(Origin Recognition\): The heterohexameric Origin Recognition Complex \(ORC1-6\) binds to) Tj
T*
(   replication origins across the genome.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 2 \(Helicase Loader Recruitment\): ORC recruits two essential licensing factors: Cdc6 \(an) Tj
T*
(   AAA+ ATPase\) and Cdt1.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 3 \(MCM2-7 Double Hexamer Loading\): Cdt1 binds the Mini-Chromosome Maintenance \(MCM2-7\)) Tj
T*
(   heterohexameric ring. Together with ORC and Cdc6, it hydrolyzes ATP to load a head-to-head) Tj
T*
(   double hexamer of MCM2-7 around double-stranded origin DNA in an inactive, sliding ring) Tj
T*
(   configuration.) Tj
T*
(   - Once loaded, origins are considered 'licensed' and competent for firing.) Tj
T*
(   2. Origin Firing at G1/S Transition:) Tj
T*
(   Activation of S-CDKs \(Cyclin E/A-CDK2\) and Dbf4-Dependent Kinase \(DDK / Cdc7\):) Tj
T*
(   - DDK phosphorylates the N-terminal tails of Mcm4 and Mcm6.) Tj
T*
(   - S-CDK phosphorylates Sld2 and Sld3, recruiting essential firing factors: Cdc45 and the GINS) Tj
T*
(   tetramer \(Sld5-Psf1-Psf2-Psf3\).) Tj
T*
(   - This transforms the dormant MCM2-7 double hexamer into two active, single-stranded CMG \(Cdc45) Tj
T*
(   - MCM2-7 - GINS\) replicative helicase complexes that separate and begin unwinding DNA) Tj
T*
(   bidirectionally.) Tj
T*
(   3. Multi-Tiered Mechanisms Preventing Re-Replication \(Once-Per-Cycle Control\):) Tj
T*
(   As cells transition into S, G2, and M phases, CDK and DDK activities rise to maximum levels,) Tj
T*
(   triggering four redundant biochemical mechanisms that destroy licensing competence until the) Tj
T*
(   next cell cycle:) Tj
T*
(   - Mechanism 1 \(Cdc6 Inactivation\): CDK phosphorylates Cdc6, exposing a nuclear export signal) Tj
T*
(   that expels Cdc6 into the cytoplasm, while residual nuclear Cdc6 is targeted for degradation.) Tj
T*
(   - Mechanism 2 \(Cdt1 Proteolysis\): Cdt1 is degraded via two distinct ubiquitin pathways: \(a\)) Tj
T*
(   During S-phase, Cdt1 binds PCNA at active replication forks and is polyubiquitinated by) Tj
T*
(   CRL4-Cdt2; \(b\) S-CDK phosphorylates Cdt1, targeting it for SCF-Skp2-mediated degradation.) Tj
T*
(   - Mechanism 3 \(Geminin Inhibition\): From S phase through early mitosis, the protein Geminin) Tj
T*
(   accumulates. Geminin binds directly to any newly synthesized Cdt1, sterically blocking its) Tj
T*
(   interaction with MCM2-7. Geminin is destroyed only at anaphase by APC/C.) Tj
T*
(   - Mechanism 4 \(ORC Phosphorylation\): CDK1/2 phosphorylate Orc1 and Orc2, causing Orc1) Tj
T*
(   dissociation from chromatin and preventing new pre-RC assembly.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
