(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the molecular etiology and chromosomal consequences of Meiotic Non-Disjunction, contrasting maternal age-dependent cohesin decay.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Etiology of Meiotic Non-Disjunction and Maternal Age-Dependent Cohesin Decay:) Tj
T*
(   1. Chromosomal Aneuploidy and Meiotic Non-Disjunction:) Tj
T*
(   Meiotic non-disjunction is the failure of paired chromosomes to segregate symmetrically during) Tj
T*
(   gametogenesis, producing gametes with missing \(monosomy, 2n-1\) or extra \(trisomy, 2n+1\)) Tj
T*
(   chromosomes:) Tj
T*
(   - Non-Disjunction in Meiosis I: Homologous chromosomes fail to separate. Yields 100% abnormal) Tj
T*
(   gametes \(50% disomic, 50% nullisomic\).) Tj
T*
(   - Non-Disjunction in Meiosis II: Sister chromatids fail to separate. Yields 50% normal haploid) Tj
T*
(   gametes, 25% disomic, and 25% nullisomic gametes.) Tj
T*
(   2. Striking Maternal Age Effect:) Tj
T*
(   Over 90% of human aneuploidies \(e.g., Trisomy 21 / Down syndrome, Trisomy 18 / Edwards) Tj
T*
(   syndrome, Trisomy 13 / Patau syndrome\) originate from errors in MATERNAL meiosis, with risk) Tj
T*
(   escalating exponentially after maternal age 35 \(1 in 1,000 at age 20 vs 1 in 30 at age 45\).) Tj
T*
(   3. The Molecular Basis: Cohesin Deterioration During Prolonged Dictyate Arrest:) Tj
T*
(   - In female human embryos, primary oocytes initiate Meiosis I during fetal development \(~5th) Tj
T*
(   month of gestation\), complete crossing over, and then enter a prolonged developmental arrest at) Tj
T*
(   the diplotene stage, termed Dictyate \(Dictyotene\) arrest.) Tj
T*
(   - Oocytes remain locked in dictyate arrest until individual follicles are recruited for) Tj
T*
(   ovulation, a dormancy lasting from 12 years up to 45-50 years!) Tj
T*
(   - Meiotic cohesin complexes containing the meiosis-specific kleisin subunit Rec8 and protection) Tj
T*
