(   translocation does NOT require ATP-fueled motor walking; instead, it directly harnesses the) Tj
T*
(   mechanical strain energy released by depolymerizing microtubule protofilaments!) Tj
T*
(   2. The Protofilament Curling Power Stroke:) Tj
T*
(   - The GDP-tubulin core of a microtubule is held in a strained, straight lattice by the GTP cap.) Tj
T*
(   - Upon cap loss, protofilaments spring outward, peeling back into curved structures with a) Tj
T*
(   radius of curvature of ~20 nm.) Tj
T*
(   - This conformational curl generates substantial mechanical force \(~30-65 pN per) Tj
T*
(   protofilament\), pushing against kinetochore couplers.) Tj
T*
(   3. The Ndc80 Complex as a Sliding Sleeve:) Tj
T*
(   - Multiple Ndc80 heterotetramers form a multivalent, oligomeric cluster surrounding the) Tj
T*
(   microtubule end.) Tj
T*
(   - Ndc80 CH-domains bind weakly and reversibly to adjacent tubulin monomers.) Tj
T*
(   - As outward-curling protofilaments peel back, they exert steric force against the Ndc80 heads,) Tj
T*
(   pushing the entire complex backward toward the minus-end without detachment \(biased diffusion /) Tj
T*
(   power stroke coupling\).) Tj
T*
(   4. The Ska1 Complex \(The Human Equivalent of the Dam1 Ring\):) Tj
T*
(   - The human Ska complex \(Ska1 - Ska2 - Ska3\) forms a V-shaped homodimer that binds directly to) Tj
T*
(   Ndc80 and possesses an unconventional curved winged-helix domain that tracks along the curved) Tj
T*
(   surface of peeling protofilaments.) Tj
T*
(   - Ska1 dramatically increases the grip of Ndc80 on depolymerizing ends, reducing the rate of) Tj
T*
(   accidental detachment by 10-fold and allowing kinetochores to pull against heavy opposing) Tj
T*
(   viscous loads \(up to 700 pN per chromosome\).) Tj
