12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Describe the molecular mechanisms of Chromosome Non-Disjunction leading to Aneuploidy and explain the resulting genomic instability in cancer.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Chromosome Non-Disjunction, Aneuploidy, and Chromosomal Instability \(CIN\) in Cancer:) Tj
T*
(   1. Mechanisms of Chromosome Non-Disjunction in Somatic Cells:) Tj
T*
(   - Merotelic Kinetochore Attachments: The most prevalent cause of somatic chromosome) Tj
T*
(   missegregation. A single kinetochore binds microtubules from both spindle poles simultaneously.) Tj
T*
(   Because both kinetochores are attached, the SAC is satisfied and anaphase proceeds; however,) Tj
T*
(   the merotelic chromosome experiences equal opposing forces, lagging at the spindle equator) Tj
T*
(   during anaphase.) Tj
T*
(   - Centrosome Amplification: Cancer cells frequently possess > 2 centrosomes \(due to failed) Tj
T*
(   cytokinesis, cell fusion, or Plk4 overexpression\). Multipolarly dividing spindles initially) Tj
T*
(   form, which then cluster into pseudo-bipolar spindles, causing an extraordinarily high rate of) Tj
T*
(   merotelic errors.) Tj
T*
(   - Defective Spindle Assembly Checkpoint: Mutations or epigenetic silencing of BUB1, BUBR1, or) Tj
T*
(   MAD2 allow cells to enter anaphase prematurely before all chromosomes are aligned.) Tj
T*
(   2. Micronuclei Formation and Chromothripsis:) Tj
T*
(   - Lagging anaphase chromosomes are excluded from the main daughter nuclei upon telophase) Tj
T*
